\begin{abstract}
This paper investigates the topology and dynamics of codimension-one foliations possessing isolated Morse singularities on smooth manifolds. We develop an local elimination technique for cancelling pairs of center-saddle and saddle-saddle singularities. Using these surgeries, we establish generalizations of classical topological rigidity results, specifically extending Haefliger's theorem on the non-existence of analytic foliations and Novikov's compact leaf theorem to the singular setting.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The global structure of codimension-one foliations on smooth manifolds stands as a foundational topic at the intersection of differential geometry, dynamical systems, and low-dimensional topology. While the theory of nonsingular (regular) foliations is well-established through the interplay of holonomy groups and fundamental groups of leaves, many natural geometric constructions—such as those arising from integrable 1-forms, level sets of smooth maps, and boundary value problems—inevitably introduce singular points. Among these, \emph{isolated Morse singularities} represent the most robust and generic class of degeneracies. 

Formally, a codimension-one foliation $\F$ with isolated Morse singularities on a smooth $n$-manifold $M$ is defined outside a discrete set $\Sigma \subset M$ by a smooth integrable distribution of hyperplanes, such that around each singular point $p \in \Sigma$, $\F$ is locally given by the level sets of a smooth Morse function $f: U \subset M \to \R$ with a non-degenerate critical point at $p$. Understanding how the presence of $\Sigma$ influences the global leaf dynamics remains a central challenge in geometric topology.

\subsection{Classical Foundations: Reeb and Eells--Kuiper}

The qualitative study of foliations began with the seminal work of Georges Reeb \cite{Reeb1952}. Reeb's construction of a smooth, non-singular codimension-one foliation on the 3-sphere $S^3$—containing a unique compact leaf homeomorphic to a 2-torus—demonstrated that global topological constraints deeply dictate leaf dynamics. Furthermore, the classical \emph{Reeb Stability Theorem} asserts that a compact leaf with finite holonomy group forces neighboring leaves to fibrate over it, establishing a fundamental local-to-global rigidity phenomenon.

When singular sets are permitted, the relationship between the topology of the manifold $M$ and the local indices of the singularities becomes tighter. Eells and Kuiper \cite{EellsKuiper1962} generalized Morse-theoretic concepts to study manifolds admitting smooth functions with very few critical points, effectively initiating the classification of singular foliations whose leaves are defined by the level sets of low-complexity functions. Their work revealed that the topology of ambient manifolds—including exotic spheres—can be completely characterized by the index structures of singular foliations defined on them. Subsequent developments by Takens \cite{Takens1968} and Camacho--Lins Neto \cite{CamachoLinsNeto1985} highlighted the structural stability and local dynamics around isolated Morse saddles and centers. However, a systematic framework for manipulating and simplifying these singular sets while preserving the global foliation structure outside the modification zone has remained incomplete.

\subsection{Main Contributions}

In this paper, we advance the structural theory of singular codimension-one foliations along two main trajectories: first, by introducing a local surgery apparatus to eliminate specific pairs of Morse singularities; second, by utilizing this mechanism to generalize two foundational milestones of foliation theory—\emph{Haefliger's Theorem} and \emph{Novikov's Compact Leaf Theorem}—to the singular setting.

\subsubsection{Elimination of Singularities}
In classical Morse theory, the Smale cancellation theorem permits the elimination of a index-$k$ and index-$(k+1)$ critical point pair connected by a unique gradient flow line. In the context of foliations, eliminating singularities requires modifying the leaf space without introducing higher-order non-Morse degeneracies or disrupting the integrability of the ambient distribution.

We formulate a local model for the cancellation of singular configuration pairs, specifically addressing the \emph{center-saddle} (index $0$ or $n$ paired with index $1$ or $n-1$) and \emph{saddle-saddle} (adjacent indices $k$ and $k+1$) interactions.

\begin{theoremMain}[Singularity Elimination]\label{thm:elimination}
Let $\F$ be a codimension-one foliation on a smooth manifold $M$ with isolated Morse singularities $\Sigma$. Suppose $p, q \in \Sigma$ form a non-degenerate center-saddle or saddle-saddle configuration connected by a simple heteroclinic leaf module. Then, there exists a smooth deformation $\F_t$ ($t \in [0,1]$) supported in an arbitrarily small neighborhood $U \supset \{p, q\}$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\F_0 = \F$;
    \item $\F_1$ is a codimension-one foliation with isolated Morse singularities $\Sigma' = \Sigma \setminus \{p, q\}$;
    \item $\F_1|_{M \setminus U} = \F|_{M \setminus U}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theoremMain}

This elimination technique serves as a powerful reduction tool, enabling one to simplify the singular locus $\Sigma$ to a minimal set dictated strictly by the global homology of $M$.

\subsubsection{Generalization of Haefliger's Theorem}
Haefliger's celebrated theorem \cite{Haefliger1962} states that a closed, simply connected manifold $M$ (or more generally, a manifold with finite fundamental group) cannot admit a smooth or real-analytic regular codimension-one foliation. The presence of Morse singularities, however, allows foliations to exist on simple manifolds (as evidenced by the standard Morse functions on $S^n$). 

By combining our elimination results with a detailed analysis of leaf holonomy near singular saddles, we prove that real-analyticity severely restricts the allowable Morse indices.

\begin{theoremMain}[Singular Haefliger-type Theorem]\label{thm:haefliger}
Let $M$ be a closed, simply connected real-analytic manifold, and let $\F$ be a real-analytic codimension-one foliation on $M$ with isolated Morse singularities $\Sigma$. If $\F$ contains no center singularities (indices $0$ or $n$), then $\F$ cannot exist. In particular, any such real-analytic foliation must possess at least one compact leaf homeomorphic to a sphere or admit a non-empty locus of center-type singularities.
\end{theoremMain}

\subsubsection{Generalization of Novikov's Compact Leaf Theorem}
Novikov's Compact Leaf Theorem \cite{Novikov1965} is a cornerstone of 3-manifold topology: it guarantees that any $C^2$ regular codimension-one foliation on a closed 3-manifold $M^3$ with $\pi_1(M^3) \neq 0$ (or on $S^3$) must contain a compact leaf (specifically, a Reeb component). 

Using the local elimination machinery (Theorem~\ref{thm:elimination}), we analyze how leaves interact with Morse singularities of index $1$ and $2$ in dimension three. We prove that isolated Morse singularities cannot "destroy" compact leaves unless they act as global topologically bounded sinks or sources (centers).

\begin{theoremMain}[Singular Novikov-type Theorem]\label{thm:novikov}
Let $M^3$ be a closed 3-manifold, and let $\F$ be a $C^2$ codimension-one foliation on $M^3$ with a finite set $\Sigma$ of isolated Morse singularities. If $\F$ contains no center singularities, then $\F$ contains a compact leaf. 
\end{theoremMain}

Theorem \ref{thm:novikov} demonstrates that saddle-type Morse singularities are insufficient to dissolve the topological closed-curve dynamics (vanishing cycles) that produce Reeb-like components in 3-manifolds.

\subsection{Organization of the Paper}
This paper is structured as follows. In Section~\ref{sec:prelim}, we set notation, review local normal forms for Morse singularities, and establish the holonomy notions on singular leaves. In Section~\ref{sec:elimination}, we construct the vector field deformations required to prove Theorem~\ref{thm:elimination}. Section~\ref{sec:haefliger} is devoted to the real-analytic setting and the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:haefliger}. Finally, in Section~\ref{sec:novikov}, we analyze vanishing cycles in the presence of Morse saddles and present the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:novikov}.

