\section{Introduction}

The global structure of regular codimension-one foliations on closed smooth manifolds has been a central theme in differential topology since the foundational work of Reeb \cite{Reeb}. Among the cornerstone achievements in this field are Haefliger's non-existence theorem for analytic foliations on simply connected manifolds \cite{Haefliger} and Novikov's compact leaf theorem for $C^2$ foliations on 3-manifolds \cite{Novikov}. However, regular foliations impose severe topological restrictions on the underlying ambient manifold. To understand broader topological phenomena, it is natural to allow singularities.

In this paper, we consider $C^\infty$ codimension-one singular foliations whose singular set consists entirely of Morse singularities. Singularities of Morse type represent the generic, most structurally stable local obstructions to regularity, arising naturally as integrable $1$-forms, level sets of Morse functions, or through degenerations of contact and foliation structures (see, e.g., \cite{Ku-Ee}). While the local model of a Morse singularity $d\left(\sum_{i=1}^k x_i^2 - \sum_{j=k+1}^n x_j^2\right) = 0$ is well understood, the global interplay between the arrangement of these singularities and the dynamic behavior of the regular leaves remains a rich and active area of research.

The primary objective of this work is twofold: first, to establish topological modification techniques for singular loci by eliminating center-saddle pairs; second, to extend the foundational global theorems of Haefliger and Novikov to the singular setting by introducing the notion of \emph{weakly stable singular components}.

\subsection*{Part 1: Singularity Arrangements and Elimination of Pairs}

In Morse theory, the topology of a manifold can be simplified by canceling critical points of adjacent indices (the classical birth-death bifurcation or Smale cancellation \cite{Smale}). In the theory of singular foliations, a similar question arises: can adjacent Morse singularities of a foliation be eliminated without disturbing the ambient leaf structure outside a compact neighborhood?

The primary local obstruction to simplifying the singular locus $\mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})$ is the presence of center (index $0$ or $n$) and saddle (index $1$ or $n-1$) singularities bound together in a coupled configuration. In the first part of this paper, we systematically analyze the global topological arrangements of such Morse singularities. We formulate exact algebraic and topological conditions under which a center-saddle pair can be canceled via a smooth deformation of the foliation.

\begin{theorem}[Informal Statement A -- Elimination of Center-Saddle Pairs]
\label{thm:intro-elimination}
Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a $C^\infty$ codimension-one Morse singular foliation on a closed manifold $M^n$. Suppose $p, q \in \mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})$ form an isolated center-saddle pair connected by a canonical trajectory of the gradient-like vector field associated with $\mathcal{F}$. If the local holonomy along the connecting leaf satisfies the vanishing cycle condition, then there exists a smooth homotopy of singular foliations $\{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t\in[0,1]}$ supported in an arbitrarily small neighborhood of the trajectory such that $\mathcal{F}_1$ agrees with $\mathcal{F}$ outside the neighborhood and $\mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F}_1) = \mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F}) \setminus \{p, q\}$.
\end{theorem}

This cancellation mechanism provides a theoretical tool for reducing the complexity of singular loci, effectively generalizing classic handle-cancellation techniques to leaf-preserving differential topology.

\subsection*{Part 2: Weakly Stable Components and Classical Generalizations}

In regular foliation theory, the existence of closed leaves or vanishing cycles dictates the global topology of the manifold. In the presence of Morse singularities, regular leaves can limit towards singular centers or saddles, breaking classical holonomy techniques. 

To bridge this gap, we introduce the concept of a \emph{weakly stable singular component}. Intuitively, a singular component $\mathcal{C} \subset M$ (comprising singular leaves and their associated Morse critical points) is weakly stable if leaves entering a distinguished neighborhood of $\mathcal{C}$ are dynamically trapped or exhibit non-trivial recurrent behavior along invariant submanifolds without requiring strict transverse attractiveness.

Equipped with this notion, we generalize two fundamental theorems of differential topology to $C^\infty$ Morse singular foliations:

\begin{theorem}[Informal Statement B -- Generalized Haefliger Theorem]
\label{thm:intro-haefliger}
Let $M^n$ be a closed, simply connected $C^\infty$ manifold, and let $\mathcal{F}$ be a codimension-one Morse singular foliation on $M$. Then $\mathcal{F}$ contains at least one weakly stable singular component of center type or a non-trivial singular vanishing cycle. In particular, $\mathcal{F}$ cannot be purely saddle-like without inducing non-trivial fundamental group dynamics on the regular stratum.
\end{theorem}

Furthermore, by analyzing the holonomy along paths passing arbitrarily close to saddle-type Morse singularities, we recover a singular analog of Novikov's Compact Leaf Theorem on 3-manifolds.

\begin{theorem}[Informal Statement C -- Generalized Novikov Theorem]
\label{thm:intro-novikov}
Let $M^3$ be a closed 3-manifold and let $\mathcal{F}$ be a $C^\infty$ codimension-one Morse singular foliation on $M$. If $\mathcal{F}$ possesses a leaf $L$ with a non-trivial vanishing cycle, or if $\pi_2(M) \neq 0$, then $\mathcal{F}$ contains either a compact regular leaf of genus $g \ge 0$, a Reeb component, or a weakly stable singular component.
\end{theorem}

These results demonstrate that "weak stability" is the natural surrogate for compactness and holonomy-invariance when moving from regular to singular foliations.

\subsection*{Organization of the Paper}

The paper is organized as follows. In Section~\ref{sec:prelims}, we fix notation and review the local geometry of $C^\infty$ Morse singularities. Sections~\ref{sec:arrangements} and \ref{sec:cancellation} comprise **Part 1**, focusing on the classification of singular arrangements and the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:intro-elimination}. Sections~\ref{sec:weak-stability} and \ref{sec:haefliger-novikov} constitute **Part 2**, where we define weakly stable singular components and establish the generalized Haefliger and Novikov theorems (Theorems~\ref{thm:intro-haefliger} and \ref{thm:intro-novikov}).

